{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A list of systems that run on a world, in order, and at the same time where they can."}],"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"title":"Creating a Schedule","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/init(_:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/init(checked:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/ScheduleBuilder","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/CyclicOrdering"],"anchor":"Creating-a-Schedule"},{"title":"Running Systems","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/run(on:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/runSerially(on:)"],"anchor":"Running-Systems"},{"title":"Ordering and Conditioning Every System","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/after(_:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/before(_:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/inSet(_:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/ambiguous(with:)","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/runIf(_:)"],"anchor":"Ordering-and-Conditioning-Every-System"},{"title":"Inspecting a Schedule","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/systems","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/names","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/ambiguities()","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Ambiguity","doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/writeDOT(to:)"],"anchor":"Inspecting-a-Schedule"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Schedule","kind":"identifier"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A schedule holds the systems of one frame or step of a simulation. You build it by listing","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the systems in a closure, and run it on a world with ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/run(on:)","type":"reference"},{"text":", which runs each system once:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["var world = World()","let ball = world.spawn(Position(x: 0, y: 100), Velocity(dx: 2, dy: 0))","","let frame = Schedule {","    Gravity()","    Movement().after(Gravity.self)","}","frame.run(on: &world)","frame.run(on: &world)","print(world[Position.self, of: ball])","\/\/ Prints \"Position(x: 4.0, y: 70.0)\""]},{"type":"heading","text":"Ordering Systems","level":3,"anchor":"Ordering-Systems"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Systems run in the order they’re written, except where an ordering constraint requires"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"otherwise. A system that must run after one written later, by "},{"code":"after(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", or before one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"written earlier, by "},{"code":"before(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", is moved to satisfy the constraint, and every other system"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"keeps its written place:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["let frame = Schedule {","    Scoring().after(Movement.self)","    Movement()","    Gravity().before(Movement.self)","}","print(frame.names)","\/\/ Prints \"[\"Gravity\", \"Movement\", \"Scoring\"]\""]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A constraint naming a type with no system in the schedule is ignored, and one naming a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"system’s own type doesn’t order the system against itself. Constraints that form a cycle,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"so that no order satisfies them all, trap; build the schedule with ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/init(checked:)","isActive":true},{"text":" to throw","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/CyclicOrdering","isActive":true},{"text":" instead.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Running Systems at the Same Time","level":3,"anchor":"Running-Systems-at-the-Same-Time"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Two systems ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"conflict"}]},{"text":" when one writes a type the other reads or writes, or when either has no","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/System\/access"},{"text":". ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/run(on:)"},{"text":" runs conflicting systems in order and the rest at the same","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"time. The world ends as though the systems had run one after another in order, except that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the commands systems queue apply when their wave finishes.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"When two conflicting systems aren’t ordered by any constraint, the order they run in rests","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only on how they happen to be written. ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Systems\/documentation\/Systems\/Schedule\/ambiguities()","type":"reference"},{"text":" lists those pairs, so a test can","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"keep a schedule free of them.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/systems\/schedule"]}],"kind":"symbol","relationshipsSections":[{"title":"Conforms To","kind":"relationships","iden